WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.596
Code review should ask direct questions about passwords.

2
00:00:03.596 --> 00:00:09.980
Are plaintext passwords ever stored, logged, returned, compared unsafely, or copied into telemetry.

3
00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:14.236
Are password fields excluded from debug output and structured logs.

4
00:00:14.236 --> 00:00:18.566
Are reset tokens generated with suitable randomness and expiration.

5
00:00:18.566 --> 00:00:22.749
Are authentication errors designed to limit account enumeration.

6
00:00:22.749 --> 00:00:27.739
Are authorization and session decisions separate from password verification.

7
00:00:27.739 --> 00:00:32.362
Dependency and configuration review are just as important as code review.

8
00:00:32.362 --> 00:00:41.902
Teams should verify that password hashing libraries are maintained, configured with appropriate parameters, and used through their recommended APIs.

9
00:00:41.902 --> 00:00:46.525
Hash parameter choices should be documented and revisited periodically.

10
00:00:46.525 --> 00:00:53.864
Configuration should prevent accidental downgrade to weak algorithms or development-only settings in production.

11
00:00:53.864 --> 00:01:02.670
Operational checks should include logs, telemetry, support workflows, database access, backup handling, and incident response runbooks.

12
00:01:02.670 --> 00:01:15.365
It is not enough for the login function to be correct if another job exports credential fields, a support screen displays sensitive data, or a debug logger captures passwords during failed authentication.

13
00:01:15.365 --> 00:01:20.355
Operations determine whether secure design survives contact with production.

14
00:01:20.355 --> 00:01:25.419
Incident response for credential exposure should be planned before it is needed.

15
00:01:25.419 --> 00:01:43.104
Teams may need to assess which credentials were exposed, whether plaintext passwords or only hashes were involved, whether hash parameters were strong, whether a pepper or secret manager was compromised, whether users require notification, and whether forced reset is appropriate.

16
00:01:43.104 --> 00:01:47.140
Preparedness reduces confusion when speed and accuracy matter.
